fujinet-go-intv-desktop -- implementation status
=================================================

Full plan: see the "fujinet-go-intv-desktop" implementation plan (jzIntv-based
FujiNet Go Desktop port; STIC/registers/MOB debugger; clickable keypad
sub-window; Linux/GNOME+KDE, Windows, macOS).

DONE (verified with a real configure + build + ctest on this machine):

  M1 -- Skeleton, vendoring, headless core
    [x] cmake/Dependencies.cmake      (intv_provide_dependency, transposed
                                        from fujinet-go-coco-desktop)
    [x] jzIntv 20200712 pinned by URL + SHA256 (cmake/Dependencies.cmake)
    [x] tools/jzintv/jzintv-fujinet.patch -- regenerated fresh from a diff of
        the pristine 20200712 zip against the local FujiNet-patched checkout
        (LF-normalized; the original hand-authored patch had drifted slightly
        -- see the patch file's own header)
    [x] cmake/StageJzIntv.cmake + tools/jzintv/patch-staged-tree.py
    [x] core/CMakeLists.txt: jzintv_core static library, $(OBJS) + the
        upstream "null" backend combination (OBJS_NULL: gfx_null.c,
        snd_null.c, event_null.c, joy_null.c, plat_null.c, main_null.c --
        USE_AS_BACKEND to omit main()). jzintv.c (the non-emscripten entry
        point) needed ZERO source changes -- verified SDL-free at the source
        level.
    [x] Two upstream gaps fixed via compile definitions (not source patches):
        - USE_SYS_IOCTL on non-Windows (plat_lib.c's Linux branch sets
          CAN_TIOCGWINSZ but not USE_SYS_IOCTL, so <sys/ioctl.h> was never
          included; macOS/FreeBSD's branches already set it)
        - src/svn_revision.c was missing from the object list (lives in
          Makefile.common's OBJS, not any subMakefile)
    [x] locutus/ is C++14 (not C, unlike the rest of jzIntv) --
        enable_language(CXX) scoped to core/CMakeLists.txt
    [x] VERIFIED: `cmake -B build -DFRONTEND=none -DWITH_FUJINET=OFF &&
        cmake --build build` succeeds; `nm`/`ldd` on the resulting
        no_sdl_link_test binary show zero SDL symbols/dependencies
        (core/tests/no_sdl_link_test.c, ctest target no_sdl_link)

  M2 -- ROM embedding + compliance
    [x] tools/jzintv/roms/{exec.bin,grom.bin} (8192 / 2048 bytes)
    [x] tools/jzintv/embed-roms.py (transposed from tools/xroar/embed-roms.py
        -- one ROM class here, not two, since EXEC and GROM are both Mattel
        copyright)
    [x] core/src/roms_embedded.h, WITH_INTV_ROMS option wired into
        core/CMakeLists.txt, core/tests/no_embedded_roms.py
    [x] COMPLIANCE.md
    [x] VERIFIED: WITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF produces an empty embedded-ROM table;
        WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON embeds both images (checked roms_embedded.c
        directly -- the end-to-end "shipped binary contains no ROM bytes"
        check needs a binary that actually references the table, which is
        M3+ scope; the ctest is already wired to run against boot_smoke and
        every built frontend once they exist)

IN PROGRESS -- M3, session library:

  [x] core/jzintv/intv_frame.{h,c} -- the frame store (XRGB8888, serial-based
      copy contract, mirrors cocosession_copy_frame). Thread-safe (pthread
      mutex): emulator thread publishes, any UI thread copies out.
  [x] core/jzintv/desktop/gfx_desktop.c -- replaces gfx_null.c in the object
      list (core/CMakeLists.txt). A close copy of gfx_null.c; the only real
      change is gfx_refresh publishing into intv_frame instead of doing
      nothing. Confirmed the STIC bakes the border color into gfx->vid itself
      (stic_push_vid in the staged tree), so there is nothing extra to
      composite here.
  [x] core/jzintv/intv_host.{h,c} -- owns cfg_t intv (a process-wide
      singleton in jzIntv itself, not something intv_host imposes) and runs
      jzintv_entry_point() on its own pthread. Provisions exec.bin/grom.bin
      from the embedded table (core/src/roms_embedded.h) into a caller-given
      ROM directory, skipping any file already there so an imported ROM
      always wins. Always passes --fujinet=host:port and no ROM argument, so
      the machine boots the embedded FujiNet config ROM until a frontend
      loads a cartridge (a later milestone). --fujinet is safe with nothing
      listening -- fujinet_init()'s only failure mode is a codec self-test,
      and fn_sock.c's connect is non-blocking -- so this needed no
      WITH_FUJINET wiring to prove out.
  [x] core/jzintv/intv_host.{h,c} -- direct pad injection
      (intv_host_pad_key/intv_host_pad_disc), bypassing jzIntv's own
      keysym/event layer entirely: writes the exact scan-code-like values
      src/cfg/mapping.c's PD0L_KP*/PD0L_A_*/PD0L_D_* bindings write into
      pad_t.l[]/r[] directly. This is the whole mechanism the clickable
      keypad window (M5) needs -- no event-subsystem plumbing required.
      12 keypad digits/Clear/Enter, 3 action buttons, all 16 disc positions
      (including the one non-obvious combo, ESE = E|SE = 0x81).
  [x] core/include/intvsession.h + core/src/{session,settings,paths}.c --
      the public, toolkit-agnostic API (settings store, XDG config/data
      paths, lifecycle, frame copy, pad injection), wrapping intv_host +
      intv_frame the way cocosession.h wraps coco_host. settings.c is a
      near-verbatim transposition of the CoCo port's (flat key=value INI,
      one store shared by every frontend).
  [x] core/tests/boot_smoke.c -- starts intv_host, polls intv_frame_copy for
      a non-blank frame, stops cleanly. VERIFIED: boots the real embedded
      config ROM and produces a non-blank frame in ~20ms.
  [x] core/tests/pad_test.c -- VERIFIED against the real running machine:
      exact scan codes for a digit/action button on both controllers (with
      cross-controller isolation checked), release-clears-to-zero, and two
      disc positions including the ESE combo.
  [x] core/tests/session_test.c -- VERIFIED end-to-end through the public API
      only: settings persist across a close/reopen of the same config dir,
      paths resolve correctly, and (when ROMs are available) start / frame
      capture / pad injection / stop all work through intvsession rather than
      intv_host directly.
  All four tests pass via ctest in both WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON and =OFF configs,
  confirmed with full clean-room rebuilds (rm -rf build third_party
  core/jzintv-generated && reconfigure) in both.

  [x] core/src/intv_keymap.c + core/include/intvsession.h's
      intvsession_key_from_keysym() -- a pure function (no session state, no
      threading) mapping a keysym to a pad_key/side or disc position/side,
      copied entry-by-entry from src/cfg/mapping.c's own default keyboard
      bindings (cfg_key_bind[], column 0): numpad -> left keypad, number row
      -> right keypad, RSHIFT/RALT/RCTRL <-> LSHIFT/LALT/LCTRL crossed to
      left/right action buttons (upstream really does cross them), arrows +
      IJKM/O/U/N/, -> left disc, DRWEASZXC -> right disc. Known, documented
      limitation: overwrites the disc position rather than OR-combining
      simultaneously-held keys the way upstream's event subsystem does, so
      there's no keyboard equivalent of holding two arrows for a diagonal
      (the 8 primary directions are all reachable through a single key
      either way, so nothing is unreachable -- only true two-key diagonals
      on the arrow-key scheme specifically). F10/F11/F12 deliberately
      excluded (reserved for frontends), as are upstream's own hotkeys
      (QUIT/RESET/SHOT/...) and its ECS-keyboard-emulation column, neither
      of which is a pad action.
  [x] core/tests/keymap_test.c -- VERIFIED: representative sample across
      every category above (numpad, number row, crossed action buttons,
      arrows, both diagonal schemes, case-insensitivity, and confirms
      non-pad keys like Q/F correctly map to nothing).
  All five tests (no_sdl_link, boot_smoke, pad_test, session, keymap) pass
  via ctest in both WITH_INTV_ROMS=ON and =OFF, confirmed with full
  clean-room rebuilds in both.

  Still open in M3: audio capture (snd_desktop.c replacing snd_null.c --
  snd_null.c's mixer runs today and does not hang, but nothing ever reclaims
  its "dirty" buffers back to "clean" without a consumer, so mixing silently
  stops once the buffer pool is exhausted; fine for the short-lived tests
  above, not for a real play session), gamepad backend, FujiNet runtime
  wiring (cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake is still a stub -- WITH_FUJINET=OFF
  only), fujibus_smoke, dasm_test, stic_test.

NOT STARTED:

  cmake/FujiNetRuntime.cmake is a stub (WITH_FUJINET=OFF only; ON is a hard
  error pointing here). Needs: fujinet-firmware pinned to the branch/commit
  carrying the Intellivision BoIP work, FUJINET_TARGET=RS232 build, bundling.

  M4 -- Debugger engine: core/include/intvdebug.h, core/debugger/{debugger,
        cp1600dasm,sticview,symbols}.c -- STIC/registers/MOB views.

  M5 -- GNOME frontend (GTK4 + libadwaita): main window, debugger window,
        keypad sub-window (both hand controllers, clickable keypad/disc/
        action buttons -- no precedent in any sibling project, build this
        one first and port its layout to the other three).

  M6 -- KDE (Qt6 Widgets), macOS (AppKit), Windows (Win32) frontends.

  M7 -- Packaging: icons, flatpak manifests, NSIS installer, CPack DEB/RPM/
        TGZ, CI workflows (linux/windows/macos/flatpak/release), all release
        builds with -DWITH_INTV_ROMS=OFF.
        Icon: same icon as the other FujiNet Go Desktop clients, but on a
        dark green (#1e4912) background (per user note, 2026-08-10).
